Appologies somehow missed that - so the answers are below:

* In providing this service, are they given access to any user personal data? Or is the service solely to translate the business content in the app? _(just making sure we don’t need a Data Processing Agreement here)_ Anything commercially sensitive?
{color:#36b37e}No access to personal data. They will get list of strings, that need to be translated for apps and web pages into target language(s) and they provide translations. Translation strings do not contain personal data. In terms of commercial sensitivity - Some translation strings might give clear hint about new functionality to be launched, that is not publicly announced yet. So this is something we should probably protect leaking from translation agency{color}
* We wouldn’t want to accept US law or jurisdiction since we have no representation in the US in case of a dispute. I will recommend something neutral such as UK.
{color:#36b37e}I guess nothing for me to answer here. It is question for Alconost{color}
* They state that they _Shall independently arrange for all work to be completed_ so we need to obligate them to be fully liable for any third parties. Currently, this is missing.
{color:#36b37e}In this case yes in case they use third parties (I believe very common in translation business), then they should be fully liable{color}
* In terms of contract performance, do you want to be able to review the completed work, make changes etc.? Since it’s not regulated at the moment you will have very little recourse _legally_ to complain if not satisfied. Consider if this warrants being clarified (depends a lot on what the cost of this service will be).
{color:#36b37e}This is tricky as we do not have internal competence to evaluate translation quality. If translations are wrong or misleading, then we most probably get first indications from the market via Customer Support. And reasons for wrong translations might not always be on service provider side. So we would probably need to agree how those complaints are handled. I suggest, that Alconost should provide initial proposal about this procedure and we can then review and discuss as necessary. Also there should be time commitment from service provider side how fast they do the translation from the point they get the  work assignment. As we have the price quote from them, then they should indicate what time commitment is included there. {color}
* What is the term of the agreement? Can we terminate at any time? This is totally missing and needs adding.
{color:#36b37e}As there is no fixed fee or exclusivity involved, but only cost element is amount of actual translation done, then termination clauses are not that critical. We can always just get similar service from someone else. But there is service continuity aspect i.e. Service provider should notify us approx 2 month in advance about termination from their side, so it gives us enough time to find replacement. So 2 month notice from either party would be sufficient clause I believe{color}
* There are no external terms referenced, so I am assuming no “general terms and conditions” apply? I will add a point confirming this.
{color:#36b37e}Yes correct.{color}
* Sorry, what does submit via “Crowdin” mean? Is this just where they supply you finished work?
{color:#36b37e}It is the software platform where actual translation workflow happens. We use the enterprise version, that enables to integrate external partners into our own translation workflow in a way, that they only see their work items and once work is done, they submit it directly into our project. Here is the link to the platform: {color}[{color:#36b37e}https://crowdin.com/enterprise{color}|https://crowdin.com/enterprise]
* Costs are not reflected. How are we charged for the service, how are fees calculated (set and project-based? Time based?), when are they be paid (at once? Installments) etc? This needs to all be included. 
{color:#36b37e}Their current pricing logic is fixed cost per 1000 translated characters (with spaces). We have the quote they have committed to. Although we have not really discussed the invoicing frequency and price change procedure. Yes that should be included into the agreement. I would suggest to have monthly invoicing cycle (i.e. invoice for all translations done last month and separated by language and project) And price changes should be communicated at least 30 days in advance.{color}

